  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nPeriods of showers and thunderstorms expected to continue underneath \nan increasingly oppressive ridge the next couple of days. A few of \nthese storms may be strong to severe, more on that in a moment.\n\nBeginning of the work week features temperatures aloft warming a \ncouple more degrees, this translates to hotter temperatures at the \nsurface during peak heating. Coupled with westerly flow advecting a \nhigher moisture content airmass, heat indices will climb to near \n100F tomorrow and into the low 100sF on Tuesday. Not out of the \nquestion to hit heat advisory criteria on Tuesday but would need \nclouds to work out and higher surface moisture than guidance \ncurrently depicts. Blended lower dewpoint values into the forecast \nsince the NBM typically overdoes dewpoints against the ASOS \ncalibrated measurements. \n\nAs far as storms, we have several potential rounds. With each round \npotentially yielding further downstream consequences for the \nforecast like a line of dominoes, just know the inherent uncertainty \nof summertime convection is still present.\n\nFirst round is overnight tonight into early Monday morning. The HRRR \nhas been the most consistent, showing for a while now the potential \nfor the overnight MCS blasting through Middle Tennessee and \nsouthward. Other CAMs have been in support in varying degrees, \nalthough there's still variance in exact timing. Other than storms\nexisting, the important question is the degree of SBCAPE \navailable to the presumed thunderstorm complex, with the HREF at \nleast suggestive of at least 1000J of CAPE available as the storm \ncomplex moves through Tennessee overnight. There's also questions\nof eastern extent, SPC recently updated the Level 1 wind risk as \nfar east as Knox and Cherokee Counties, which the NAM would agree \nwith. The more likely locations lie along the Cumberland Plateau. \nShould the surface inversion not be too strong, gusty winds will\noccur, along with brief heavy rainfall.\n\nOnce the morning storms move out Monday itself is likely to be quiet \nwith no forcing or lost MCS wandering through. The next storm \ncomplex may, or may not occur, on Tuesday morning. Some guidance, \nlike the RRFS and the high res NAM, depict the Tuesday MCS dying on \nour door mat along the Kentucky/Virginia borders. Contrast this with \nthe 12z HRRR which brings another MCS clean into the bulk of the \nregion Tuesday morning. This system, if materialized, would also \nhave a risk for wet gusty winds and heavy rains. It will also play a \nsignificant role in the degree to which we'll be able to \ndestabilize heading into Tuesday afternoon. \n\nDepending on remaining cloud debris on Tuesday, and the amount of \nsunny skies we can recover back to, will help determine how much \nCAPE can build in the summer skies ahead of the incoming cold front. \nKinematically there's a lot going in favor of severe storms on \nTuesday, especially the further northeast you go. Deep layer bulk \nshear is 40 to 50 knots, which given this time of year is the \nclimatological minimum for shear, means we have plenty of shear. The \nGFS doesn't seem too impressed, which is likely chalked up to\ncomplications from morning storms. Should the area have \neverything line up with timing of the front, the strong \nkinematics, and ample amounts of CAPE heading into Tuesday \nafternoon, then we'll be primed for an all-hazard threat profile. \nTornado is the lower end of the risks, also the easiest to get \nwiped out depending on what happens Tuesday morning, but the 0-1 \nshear profiles and possibility of fully mixing back out means we \nhave to consider it. If we get strong and deep supercells, then \nsevere hail in northeastern TN into southwestern VA is likely. \nDamaging winds is the main risk, with guidance depicting enough \ndry air between 700 and 500 mb plus a fairly saturated profile \noverall to prompt DCAPE to above 1000J, indicating severe wind \nrisks. Timing for the main threat of the week would be Tuesday \nafternoon into Tuesday evening, possibly a fairly narrow window \nonce we get closer.\n\nAfter Tuesday the ridge trough pattern sharpens up briefly but more \nimportantly a slightly milder and drier summer airmass moves in. \nWednesday through Friday look mostly dry rain wise, before a new \ntrough starts looking to come this way to start August off when we\nhit next weekend, bringing back rain and storm chances.",
